# composition.spire — the composition, as a person reviews it.
#
# This file is the **source of truth**. Open it, edit it, review it in a pull request; `git diff` on it
# is a diff of the design. `SPIRE.application.json` is the *generated record* the fill phase reads back,
# and it is the same spec in another form — so if the two ever disagree, this file wins.
#
# It is YAML, which is why it can carry these comments and why it reads as data rather than machinery.
# JSON is a subset of YAML, so a JSON answer parses here too.
#
# Read it with `application_spec::parse_composition`. It is *strict*: a field of the wrong type or a
# block that is not syntactically correct is refused, with the line and column that caused it.
#
# The field-by-field reference is `docs/composition-format.md`; this file is that format filled in, with
# the reasoning beside the lines it explains.

# The application **in English**: what it is for and what it does, in the person's own words.
#
# This is the half of a composition that is not a graph, and it is here rather than in a separate document
# so the structure and the sentence explaining it cannot drift apart. It is *prose* — no rule reads it,
# and it carries no computation (a guard, an arithmetic step, a page transition belongs in the unit's
# code, in the language that has to run it). It travels into the generated tree **verbatim**: the whole
# thing becomes the header of `main/`, and each unit's own `description` (below) becomes that unit's class
# doc-comment, so the code is written against the same words the design was reviewed against.
description: |
  A room air-quality monitor. It samples particulate matter and temperature/humidity once a second, keeps
  a short rolling average and shows it on the board's screen. A tap pages between the live reading, a
  trend and an about screen; a long press re-baselines the average. It runs from the battery and holds the
  last reading across a power cycle.

framework: actors
justification: a touch UI, a reading a second and a battery gauge — interactive, human timescale

board:
  chip: esp32s3
  bsp: m5stack_core_s3
  hal: m5unified

# The **resources** this application composes against: the devices it has, declared rather than assumed.
# A unit names one of them in `uses`, and a device nobody declared is a device the application does not
# have — which is the rule that stops a composition inventing hardware.
#
# Each declared resource has **exactly one owner**: the unit whose `uses` names it, the actor that holds
# the device in its state. Every other unit that needs it reaches it by **messaging that owner**, so a
# second `uses` of the same device is refused. Ownership is state; access is messages — a device is
# owned, an algorithm is shared (a plain `library` component may be named by several units).
#
# `roles` is what the device *is*, as the capabilities the vocabulary knows — a **list**, because a part
# may be more than one at once (the SHT20 below is temperature *and* humidity). `via` is where the code
# that talks
# to it comes from:
#   board     — a part of the board: the BSP reaches it, so no component of this project drives it;
#   library   — driven by a component of this project, whose `id` *is* the resource name;
#   published — driven by a managed dependency the manifest resolves from the registry.
#
# `bus`/`address` are written when the part has them: the wiring function its literal in `main/` is
# written from, and the address the driver is given — one device, one address, stated once. Addresses
# are quoted as a house rule: this reader keeps a scalar's text in a string field either way, but a
# quoted address means the same thing in every YAML parser and reads as text, not as a number.
resources:
  - name: display
    roles: [media.display]
    via: board

  - name: touch_panel
    roles: [media.touch]
    via: board
    bus: i2c_internal
    address: "0x38"

  - name: battery
    roles: [power.battery]
    via: board

  # A driver-backed device shares its name with its driver on purpose — one device, one word: `sps30` and
  # `sht20` are the two components below, and this address is the one the driver is given in `main/`.
  - name: sps30
    roles: [sensing.particulate]
    via: library
    bus: i2c
    address: "0x69"

  - name: sht20
    roles: [sensing.temperature, sensing.humidity]
    via: library
    bus: i2c
    address: "0x40"

# The **messages**: the shapes the design states once, in one list (the way `units` holds components and
# actors). A declaration is one of two things, never both:
#   a **type** — an `id` and its `fields`: the members that cross one edge, written as the `struct` the
#     scaffold generates, so every actor that sends or receives it compiles against the same shape;
#   a **set** — an `id` and its `kinds`: the several kinds one mailbox carries, written as a
#     `std::variant` over them, so an actor that reacts to several things is still one mailbox and one
#     `on_message`, dispatching on which kind arrived.
#
# A type's `fields` are `{ name, type?, description? }`. The `name` becomes the struct member, the
# `description` the `///` above it, and the `type` is carried **verbatim** into the stub — a hint about
# the device, protocol or screen at the other end, never a checked type: only the declaration's `id` and
# each field's `name` are validated. A field with no `type` is a name the design states and nothing more,
# so the scaffold leaves the `TODO` rather than guessing the member.
#
# A declaration is **shared**: it is named by an actor's `message`, by a stage's port, or by one of a
# set's `kinds` — the type belongs to the edge, not to either end. `Reading` below is declared as a type
# *and* named as a kind of `screen_update` *and* as `air_quality`'s own message: one shape, spoken three
# ways. `Gesture` is the same sharing without the declared shape — a kind here *and* `touch`'s `message`,
# still a stub the fill writes.
messages:
  - id: Reading                    # a type — the shape of the reading air_quality posts to view
    description: one SPS30 sample, as the driver reported it.
    fields:
      - name: pm_2_5
        type: uint16
        description: PM2.5 as a raw count, µg/m³
      - name: pm_10
        type: uint16

  - id: screen_update              # a set — the several kinds one mailbox carries
    kinds: [Reading, Gesture]
    description: what the view answers — a reading to paint, or a gesture to turn the page.

units:
  # A **unit**'s `description` is the slice of the application's English that belongs to it — what this
  # actor (or driver, or stage) does, in the same voice as the whole-application prose above. It becomes
  # that unit's generated class doc-comment, so each file carries the sentence that explains it. Optional:
  # a driver's `provides` line is usually enough, but an actor almost always has one, because "what does
  # this actor do on receiving X" is a question its message type and state hint cannot answer.
  #
  # A component is framework-agnostic — a driver on a bus, or pure code. It carries no message and no
  # state, which is what keeps it usable under either framework.
  - id: sps30
    kind: component
    role: driver
    source: stub
    provides: PM1/2.5/4/10 readings
    bus: i2c

  - id: sht20
    kind: component
    role: driver
    source: stub
    provides: temperature and humidity
    bus: i2c

  - id: rolling_average
    kind: component
    role: library
    source: existing
    provides: a rolling window

  # An actor is the application's own composition: a message, the state behind it, and the refs it sends
  # to. Facet annotations ride on the unit — `sleeps` and `wake` here are the *power* facet, because a
  # facet is a lens over the composition and converges into units rather than owning an artifact.
  - id: sampler
    kind: actor
    message: Tick
    description: wakes on the timer, reads both sensors and posts the reading on.
    state: reads both sensors
    uses: [sps30, sht20]
    sends_to: [air_quality]
    sleeps: true
    wake: timer@1s

  - id: air_quality
    kind: actor
    message: Reading
    description: folds each reading into the rolling average and posts it to the view.
    state: the rolling average
    uses: [rolling_average]
    sends_to: [view]

  - id: view
    kind: actor
    # The view reacts to *two* kinds of thing — the reading `air_quality` posts across the edge and the
    # gesture `touch` sends — so its `message` names the **set** declared above rather than one struct.
    # One actor, one mailbox, one `on_message`; the kinds are the shared types the set lists.
    message: screen_update
    description: paints the reading and pages between the three screens on a gesture.
    state: the LVGL screen
    # The board's screen is the view's: it holds the display in its state. A `board` part is reached by
    # `uses` with no component in between — and it has one owner, which is what makes ownership checkable.
    uses: [display]

  - id: touch
    kind: actor
    message: Gesture
    description: turns a tap into a page and a long press into a re-baseline.
    state: menu and calibration
    uses: [touch_panel]
    sends_to: [view, sampler]

  # The battery gauge. Its *unit id* is `power`; the **facet** block near the end is also called `power`.
  # They are different planes — a unit and a lens — and both names are legal.
  - id: power
    kind: actor
    message: Tick
    description: reads the battery gauge and reports it to the view.
    state: the battery gauge
    uses: [battery]
    sends_to: [view]

# `from -> to`, by unit id. Stated rather than derived, because the edges are what a reviewer checks.
wiring:
  - sampler -> air_quality
  - air_quality -> view
  - touch -> view
  - touch -> sampler
  - power -> view

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The facets: power, storage, networking and security. Each is optional, each is decided and checked
# differently, and none of them is a separate artifact — they are lenses over the composition above.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# What a unit does for a facet is an annotation on that unit (`sleeps`, `storage_role`, `network_role`);
# only the product-wide facts live here.
power:
  budget_mah: 2000
  target_days: 30

storage:
  medium: flash
  retention: until_uploaded

network:
  transport: wifi
  provision: softap

security:
  requires: [tls]
